Z||000000||Eric||12-17-1999||07:17 PM||||Okay, I've got it, so now what?  The nice little regulator came from the Fierostore in a cute little Hypertech box WITH NO INSTRUCTIONS!!!  I can see it's a simple unit to install.  It looks like the stock one but it also has a screw on top (adjusts fuel pressure in some way).  It also has a piece of metal that's about the size of a quarter lose that came with the unit (I don't know what this does).  And to top it off, there was a set of gaskets that go to a V8 inside the box.  Man, I hope this works.  I gonna put this thing in tonight, so if anybody knows anything about it please tell me. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Eric||12-17-1999||07:28 PM||||Now I know a little more.  A guage needs to be used to get an idea of how much pressure you're dialing in and the gaskets go to car the kit was intended for (not our V6's).  I still am not clear on how this thing works.  I hate not knowing how things work!||||reg||8
Z||000002||HF6582||12-17-1999||07:54 PM||||Eric,<P>The screw on the top is for pressure adj.<BR>Turn it clockwise for more pressure.<BR>Turn it counterclockwise for less pressure.<BR>And you really do need a guage when it is installed.<BR>Good Luck<BR>Scott||||reg||2
Z||000003||Shaun41178||12-17-1999||07:54 PM||||Here is what you need to do.  First before removing the old regulator take the measurement with a fuel pressure gauge to see what the factory one was set at.  After all you don't want the new one to be at the same pressure or less pressure then the stock one.<P>Then install the new fuel regulator and set it at 5psi above the one you took off.  It is a good start and then go from there.  Of course without an air/fuel gauge or a pyrometer you won't be able to tell if you are too rich or still lean.<P>Good luck and let us know how the install goes and keep us updated on how well she runs||||reg||1
